exemplar.en:
Defects and evidence. (1) The 'setSort' case spreads state without resetting page, so the supplied observation returns page 2 for the unchanged key 'sizeKiB' and direction 'asc' although the contract requires page 1 for every setSort. (2) The 'setStatus' case returns state unchanged, so 'all' page 3 with pageSize 2 changing to 'ready' returns page 3 although the filtered total is 4 and lastPage is 2. (3) The comparator has no id tie-break, so with status 'ready' and descending sizeKiB, r-201 and r-203 both have sizeKiB 140 and the CSV order leaves r-203 first, while the contract requires r-201 first because equal keys always order by id ascending in both directions.

Corrected complete module, one self-contained file, no imports:

```ts
export type Row = { id: string; name: string; sizeKiB: number; status: 'ready' | 'queued' };
export type SortKey = 'id' | 'sizeKiB';
export type StatusFilter = 'all' | 'ready' | 'queued';
export type TableState = { sortKey: SortKey; sortDir: 'asc' | 'desc'; status: StatusFilter; page: number; pageSize: number };
export type Action =
  | { type: 'setSort'; key: SortKey; dir: 'asc' | 'desc' }
  | { type: 'setStatus'; status: StatusFilter }
  | { type: 'setPage'; page: number };

export const ROWS: Row[] = [
  { id: 'r-204', name: 'Delta bundle', sizeKiB: 60, status: 'ready' },
  { id: 'r-203', name: 'Cobalt bundle', sizeKiB: 140, status: 'ready' },
  { id: 'r-205', name: 'Echo bundle', sizeKiB: 90, status: 'ready' },
  { id: 'r-201', name: 'Atlas bundle', sizeKiB: 140, status: 'ready' },
  { id: 'r-202', name: 'Beacon bundle', sizeKiB: 90, status: 'queued' },
];

export function lastPage(state: TableState): number {
  const total = ROWS.filter((r) => state.status === 'all' || r.status === state.status).length;
  return Math.max(1, Math.ceil(total / state.pageSize));
}

export function compareRows(a: Row, b: Row, state: TableState): number {
  const primary = state.sortKey === 'sizeKiB' ? a.sizeKiB - b.sizeKiB : a.id < b.id ? -1 : a.id > b.id ? 1 : 0;
  const directed = state.sortDir === 'asc' ? primary : -primary;
  return directed !== 0 ? directed : a.id < b.id ? -1 : a.id > b.id ? 1 : 0;
}

export function reduceTableState(state: TableState, action: Action): TableState {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'setSort':
      return { ...state, sortKey: action.key, sortDir: action.dir, page: 1 };
    case 'setStatus': {
      const next: TableState = { ...state, status: action.status };
      return { ...next, page: Math.min(next.page, lastPage(next)) };
    }
    case 'setPage':
      return { ...state, page: action.page };
  }
}

export function resolveTableState(state: TableState) {
  const filtered = ROWS.filter((r) => state.status === 'all' || r.status === state.status);
  const sorted = [...filtered].sort((a, b) => compareRows(a, b, state));
  const start = (state.page - 1) * state.pageSize;
  return { total: sorted.length, rows: sorted.slice(start, start + state.pageSize) };
}
```

Tests, pageSize 2 unless stated: (1) descending sizeKiB with status 'ready' returns r-201, r-203, r-205, r-204. (2) ascending sizeKiB with status 'ready' returns r-204 first and r-201 before r-203. (3) any setSort returns page 1. (4) setStatus from 'all' page 3 to 'ready' returns page 2 because total is 4 and lastPage is 2. (5) setStatus to 'queued' returns page 1 because total is 1. (6) setStatus with page 2 and status 'ready' keeps page 2. (7) setPage 99 returns page 99 and ROWS stays unmutated. This is a fictional Oursky inspired simulation, not endorsed by Oursky, and no code was executed. Next step: the fictional reviewer Marco Lee reruns checks (1) to (7).

