Run-01 full-suite data shows contamination. t1 passes at 1000 ms. t2 observed 1500 ms while expected 500 ms. t3 observed 1700 ms while expected 200 ms. Isolated runs run-02 and run-03 pass, so the clock object leaks between tests. Root cause: clock is created once at module scope with const clock = makeClockFixture() and advance mutates that shared object. The policy prohibits module-level mutable state. Required fixture contract: each test must obtain a fresh clock from makeClockFixture() inside beforeEach, with the clock variable declared inside the describe callback. Resetting in afterEach or at test start is insufficient. Inline clock objects are not permitted because the factory supplies required metadata. Corrected code:

```js
const { makeClockFixture, advance, readClock } = require('./clock');

describe('clock', () => {
  let clock;

  beforeEach(() => {
    clock = makeClockFixture();
  });

  test('t1 advances by 1000 ms', () => {
    advance(clock, 1000);
    expect(readClock(clock)).toBe(1000);
  });

  test('t2 advances by 500 ms', () => {
    advance(clock, 500);
    expect(readClock(clock)).toBe(500);
  });

  test('t3 advances by 200 ms', () => {
    advance(clock, 200);
    expect(readClock(clock)).toBe(200);
  });
});
```

Verification: rerun full suite and confirm all observed_now_ms equal expected_now_ms. Run each test in isolation. Add a regression test asserting two consecutive tests get different clock objects. Escalate to Ada Cheng if failures persist. This is a simulated assessment.

